<Base title="Scanner code lookup tools" description="Use the scanner-code finder and a compact checklist for the vehicle context needed before diagnosis." breadcrumbs={[{label:'Home',href:'/'},{label:'Tools'}]}><article class="container prose-page"><header class="page-head"><p class="eyebrow">KEEP THE USEFUL DETAILS</p><h1>Bring context to the code.</h1><p class="lede">The code finder accepts complete scanner text and recognizes standard-format code displays, including alphanumeric codes such as P0A80.</p><a class="button" href="/search/">Open code finder</a></header><section id="scan-report" class="prose"><h2>Scan-report checklist</h2><ul><li>Exact code, including any suffix.</li><li>Reporting module and code status.</li><li>Vehicle make, model, year and powertrain.</li><li>Warning messages, observed symptoms and available freeze-frame data.</li><li>Recent work or conditions that may help the technician understand the sequence.</li></ul><p>Keep personal information and VINs out of public search fields. Vehicle-specific information belongs in the appropriate manufacturer or repair workflow.</p></section><section class="prose"><h2>What the lookup does</h2><p>The search locates existing reference entries. It does not decode every manufacturer-native display, infer model compatibility, or diagnose a vehicle from a code alone.</p><a href="/reference/error-code-types/">Understand reference scopes</a></section></article></Base>
